{"agent":"Monster3-fix-size-agents-7267fe","data":{"by":"Qwen/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP8","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":3,"detail":"The new innocent-word handling deletes the matched text from the lower-cased string, which can concatenate the characters before and after the deleted word into a *new* agent literal. For example, with the production `words` set containing `claude`, the source line `x = \"cldeclineaude\"` has no `claude` substring, but `v.lower()` is `cldeclineaude`; `INNOCENT_WORDS.sub(\"\", v.lower())` removes the embedded `decline` and yields `claude`, so `substring_sites` returns `[1]`. That is a false substring site introduced by the “fix”, and it can inflate the substring ratchet total or otherwise make the test report a wrong value.","digest":"2ba040fcb55d3a5e","family":"alibaba","location":"tests/test_agent_literals_substring.py:43 (`substring_sites` / `INNOCENT_WORDS.sub`)","malformed":"","reviewer":"[REDACTED:name]","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_agent_literals_substring.py:43 (`substring_sites` / `INNOCENT_WORDS.sub`)"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"`INNOCENT_WORDS = re.compile(r\"decline[ds]?|declining\")` is hardcoded to strip specifically the word \"decline\" and its inflections, but any other English word containing the agent fragment `cline` (such as \"reclines\", \"reclining\", \"declination\", \"inclined\", \"disinclined\") will still trigger a substring match and falsely count as an agent literal. Given input `x = \"she reclined on the sofa\"`, `substring_sites` returns `[1]` because `\"reclined\"` contains `\"cline\"` and is not stripped by `INNOCENT_WORDS`.","digest":"474d22bb450d11d8","family":"google","location":"tests/test_agent_literals_substring.py:25","malformed":"","reviewer":"gemini-3.5-flash-lite","severity":"HIGH","title":"tests/test_agent_literals_substring.py:25"}],"chunks_total":1,"combined_gates":["rubber_duck","critic"],"coverage":"1/1 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":8713,"diff_sha":"632ba599aa29